---
import LegalLayout from '../layouts/LegalLayout.astro';
import site from '../config/site';
---

<LegalLayout
  title="Accessibility"
  description={`How ${site.domain} is built for accessibility — WCAG 2.2 AA contrast, keyboard navigation, reduced-motion support and screen-reader compatibility. Tell us if something doesn't work for you.`}
  path="/accessibility/"
  h1="Accessibility"
  lede="This site is built for people who are, on average, over 65. Making it genuinely usable is not a compliance checkbox — it is the job."
  updated="August 2, 2026"
>
  <h2>What we've done</h2>

  <h3>Motion</h3>
  <p>
    This site has animated elements — a soft light effect behind the content, headings that reveal
    as you scroll, numbers that count up. All of it is decorative.
  </p>
  <p>
    If your device is set to <strong>reduce motion</strong>, every one of those is switched off
    automatically. The background renders as a single still frame, all text is visible immediately
    with no reveal, numbers show their final value, and the scroll progress bar is removed
    entirely. Nothing moves and nothing is lost.
  </p>
  <p>
    You can turn this on at the system level: <em>System Settings → Accessibility → Display →
    Reduce Motion</em> on macOS and iOS, or <em>Settings → Accessibility → Visual Effects</em> on
    Windows and Android. It applies to every site that respects it, not just this one.
  </p>

  <h3>Contrast and text</h3>
  <p>
    Text and background combinations meet or exceed the WCAG 2.2 AA contrast ratio of 4.5:1 for
    body text and 3:1 for large text. The animated background is deliberately dialed down
    section-by-section so that it never sits behind text at an intensity that would compromise
    legibility — article pages run it at roughly a fifth of the intensity used on the home page.
  </p>
  <p>
    Text sizes are set in relative units, so if you have increased your browser's default font size
    the whole layout scales with it rather than clipping. The site is usable at 200% zoom without
    horizontal scrolling.
  </p>

  <h3>Keyboard and screen readers</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Every interactive element is reachable and operable by keyboard.</li>
    <li>Focus indicators are visible and high-contrast — we never remove focus outlines.</li>
    <li>A "skip to main content" link is the first thing a keyboard user reaches on every page.</li>
    <li>Headings follow a proper hierarchy so screen-reader users can navigate by structure.</li>
    <li>Form fields have real labels, and errors are announced rather than shown only in color.</li>
    <li>
      Decorative graphics are hidden from assistive technology; meaningful ones carry text
      alternatives.
    </li>
    <li>
      In the multi-step tools, focus moves to each new question so keyboard and screen-reader users
      are never left behind on the previous step.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Without JavaScript</h3>
  <p>
    Every page of content on this site is fully readable with JavaScript disabled. Animation simply
    does not run, and all content renders in its final visible state. The interactive calculators
    do need JavaScript — where that is the case, the page says so and offers a phone number
    instead.
  </p>

  <h2>Where we fall short</h2>
  <p>
    Being honest about the gaps is more useful than claiming there are none:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Some tool results are dense blocks of text. They are readable, but a screen-reader user will
      hear a lot at once.
    </li>
    <li>
      The comparison table on the Medicare Plans page scrolls sideways on narrow screens. It is
      keyboard-scrollable and properly marked up with row and column headers, but a wide table is
      never ideal on a phone.
    </li>
    <li>
      We have tested with keyboard navigation, VoiceOver and browser zoom. We have not done a
      formal third-party audit.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Tell us if something doesn't work</h2>
  <p>
    If any part of this site is difficult or impossible for you to use, please tell us. It is a bug
    and we want to fix it.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Call <a href={site.phone.href}>{site.phone.display}</a></li>
    <li>Email <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a></li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    And to be completely clear: <strong>you never have to use this website to work with us.</strong>
    Everything here can be done by phone. If the easiest thing is to call and have somebody talk you
    through it, that is a perfectly good way to do this — arguably the better one.
  </p>

  <h2>Standard we aim for</h2>
  <p>
    We aim to conform to the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.2 at Level AA. We treat
    that as a floor rather than a finish line.
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
